1: # P3K.34. Proved: the OS49 component line, both signs, and every fan
2: 
3: Root independently passed the complete proof, and blind_control independently
4: passed the signed boundary and normal comparison in Sections 2--4. The input is the actual positive-diagonal OS49 family of P3F.31--32, with
5: `lambda=r^m`, `m=3k`, `k>0`, cap vector
6: `n(1,-1,0,0)+d delta`, `n=+1` or `-1`, and arbitrary integral `d`.
7: We retain the component-supported line of multidegree `(-1,+1)` on the
8: source `I_2`. No whole-fibre height substitution is made.
9: 
10: In the initial positive-diagonal Cox product we obtain
11: \[
12:  k_{\rm Jin}=-6k,\qquad \ell_{\rm Jin}=-24k,\qquad
13:  (m_{\rm rel},n_{\rm rel},s)=(6k,0,0).
14:  \tag{OC.1}
15: \]
16: Both norm signs have the same pair for the positive physical `r`-loop.
17: For an actual allowed fan `T(v,w)`, write the physical delta cocharacter
18: as `delta=a v+b w` and put `S=a^2-ab+b^2`. The resulting return is
19: \[
20:  [\Sigma_{\Phi,T}]
21:        =\frac{3k}{4}(S-1)(S-3)\pmod {28}.
22:  \tag{OC.2}
23: \]
24: All details establishing the signed initial product, rather than only its
25: zero index, are given below.
26: 
27: ## 1. Literal origins and the simultaneous bounded Cox product
28: 
29: We use P3F.32's actual periods, not a relabelled scalar parameter:
30: \[
31:  u=(2s,s)^t+U(q),\quad
32:  \gamma=(-3s,-s+mv)^t+C(q),\quad D=3U+2C,
33: \]
34: \[
35:  H=-u-\gamma,\qquad K=3u+2\gamma=D+(s+2mv)\delta,
36:  \qquad \lambda=r^m.
37:  \tag{OC.3}
38: \]
39: Here `s=log q/(2 pi i)` and `v=log r/(2 pi i)`. In the actual physical
40: cocharacter lattice the tropical vectors of `H,K` are `(1,0),(0,1)`.
41: The period lattice generated by `(2,1),(-3,-1)` has determinant one.
42: On the auxiliary `2m`-root the two actual origins are
43: \[
44:  \sigma=nvD+nmv(v-1)\delta,\qquad
45:  o=-\frac{nsD}{2m}-\frac{ns^2}{4m}\delta-nsv\delta.
46: \]
47: Their differences are exactly
48: \[
49:  (\sigma-o)(v+\theta)- (\sigma-o)(v)
50:        =n\theta K+nm\theta(\theta-1)\delta,
51: \]
52: \[
53:  (\sigma-o)(s+2m,v)- (\sigma-o)(s,v)=nK+nm\delta.
54:  \tag{OC.4}
55: \]
56: The ordinary closing is multiplication by `t^n=q^{-n}`, and
57: `sigma(v+1)-sigma(v)=nK-ns delta`; hence the actual ordinary endpoint
58: minus the cusp-origin endpoint is the integral period `-nK`. This
59: retains the supplied source-root descent and all endpoint scalar factors.
60: 
61: Put `U_K=exp(2 pi i D_1)` and `W_K=exp(2 pi i D_2)`. At an initial
62: parameter `lambda_0=r_0^m`, the bounded angular map determined by this
63: origin is literally
64: \[
65:  C_\theta:\quad
66:  (Z,V)\longmapsto
67:  \left(U_K^{n\theta}Z,
68:        (W_K\lambda_0^2)^{n\theta}
69:        e^{2\pi i nm\theta(\theta-1)}V\right).
70:  \tag{OC.5}
71: \]
72: All powers use the actual logarithms of the nonzero units. At the
73: endpoint this is the bounded `nK` period after reindexing the cell by its
74: integral vertical shift. The scalar quadratic factor is the actual
75: physical delta action. We keep it as a circle gauge; in the orbit normal
76: three-plane it acts identically.
77: 
78: For clarity, this product closes on all three curve charts at once.
79: In the four-coordinate Cox model of K17, after the period-cell reindexing,
80: the return has multipliers
81: \[
82:  (z_0,z_4,z_1,z_2)\longmapsto
83:  ((ab\lambda_0^{2n})^{-1}z_0,z_4,az_1,
84:                      b\lambda_0^{2n}z_2),
85:  \tag{OC.6}
86: \]
87: where `a,b` are the retained signed bounded period units. The product
88: of the four displayed coordinate multipliers is one, so the same map fixes `q=z_0z_4z_1z_2` in every chart. The
89: period lattice acts freely and transitively on cells. Thus no residual
90: cell permutation or finite stabilizer is hidden in (OC.6). The same
91: interval of multipliers as in (OC.5), including any odd closed Cox loop,
92: is used on the two triple charts and all three curve tubes. The finite
93: cap's additional character `exp(2 pi i d/m)` is fixed in `r`; it adds no
94: parameter difference and remains part of the line gluing in Section 5.
95: 
96: ## 2. The mixed boundary coordinates and the exact Thom sign
97: 
98: We distinguish a cycle from its point angle. Let `(x,y)` be the point
99: angles in the ordered cycles `(u,gamma)`, and `(h,kappa)` those in
100: `(H,K)`. Equation (OC.3) gives the actual torus isomorphism
101: \[
102:  \binom h\kappa=
103:  \begin{pmatrix}2&-3\\1&-1\end{pmatrix}\binom xy,
104:  \qquad
105:  \binom xy=
106:  \begin{pmatrix}-1&3\\-1&2\end{pmatrix}\binom h\kappa.
107:  \tag{OC.7}
108: \]
109: The other source cycle is `w`; it is unchanged. These are pointwise
110: torus maps, with unimodular matrices, in the actual incoming collar.
111: 
112: The marked finite-cap map of the signed page calculation applies to
113: this cap without changing its elliptic or line action. Its norm is
114: `m y-n alpha`, where `alpha` is the finite base meridian. The explicit
115: solid-torus map (SCP.1) therefore gives the positive weighted Hopf
116: angle `beta=n y`. Its sheet-zero graph is the actual holomorphic root
117: slice for either sign, so the page has its complex orientation and
118: boundary orientation `or(E_u) d beta`.
119: 
120: Let the incoming compression graph supplied by F31 be
121: `kappa=g(h,w)`, where `g` is a globally real function. The actual positive
122: parameter loop translates this graph by `n theta` in the K direction.
123: Its inverse angular coordinate is
124: \[
125:  \theta=n(\kappa-g(h,w)).
126:  \tag{OC.8}
127: \]
128: Combining (OC.7) with `y=n beta`, the boundary map is the linear torus
129: isomorphism
130: \[
131:  \binom h\theta=
132:  \begin{pmatrix}2&-3n\\ n&-1\end{pmatrix}\binom x\beta
133:                   -\binom0{n g(h,w)}.
134:  \tag{OC.9}
135: \]
136: Its determinant is `-2+3n^2=1`, for both signs; the graph shear also
137: has determinant one. The `u` and `H` source orientations agree with
138: their complex source orientations: their Tate imaginary periods have
139: leading terms `2 Im(s)` and `Im(s)`, respectively, both positive, and
140: the retained bounded units cannot reverse this sign on the chosen
141: small collar. Keeping `w` first, (OC.9) proves the literal orientation
142: identity
143: \[
144:  \partial V_m\longrightarrow E_H\times S^1_\theta
145:                   \quad\hbox{has degree }+1
146:  \tag{OC.10}
147: \]
148: for `n=+1` and `n=-1` separately.
149: 
150: Orient the actual real compression by `partial W=F-E_H`. Then the
